Revisiting Toffler's "Future Shock" Through the Lens of Post's "Etiquette"

This now rather mild and quaint-sounding excerpt from Toffler is magnified, even more strangely (though what could be more necessary to hear in a "social media era" with its anti-social amplified ramblings?), by the cutting and too-often-ignored insights of Ms. Post: 

“To survive, to avert what we have termed future shock, the individual must become infinitely more adaptable and capable than ever before. We must search out totally new ways to anchor ourselves, for all the old roots - religion, nation, community, family, or profession - are now shaking under the hurricane impact of the accelerative thrust.” -Alvin Toffler, Future Shock

"Do not expose your private affairs, feelings or innermost thoughts in public. You are knocking down the walls of your house when you do." - Emily Post, Etiquette

Strange bedfellows? Maybe not after all. But I’m glad for their sakes neither lived to witness nor even accurately forecast the precise depth of “impoverishment” that followed their generations in the area of “human composure”. 
